6 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16c9d9f3-b6b3-5f3f-a06a-81ddff866ad4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486356 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-18{x}^{2}}{12p{x}^{2}-3x+9} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{18{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{12p{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-18)}{(12p-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-18}{12p-0+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2p} & =-2 \\\\ 4p & =3 \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486362 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486362 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486362 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486362 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486362 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d359bbd-ffea-5e49-a7be-f90cdd60255a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486362 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486364 #edb/ref 1071 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{-1}{3{x}^{3}+5}$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22cc12b8-5ddc-5c5a-b8ac-2ac46afb7003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486364 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-1}{3{x}^{3}+5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-1}{{x}^{3}})}{(3+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{3+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486372 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{27}{{x}^{3}-8}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37a7b0c0-d000-5746-9972-d4929172a0ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486372 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{27}{{x}^{3}-8} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{27}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{27}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{(1-0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486389 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486389 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{2{x}^{9}+{x}^{4}+9x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d186d1a-cd8d-5a4f-8dee-02515d4d8947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486389 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{9}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{2{x}^{9}+{x}^{4}+9x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{{x}^{9}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{9}}{{x}^{9}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{9}}+\\frac{9x}{{x}^{9}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{8}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2+0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486407 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486407 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486407 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486407 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+1}{3{x}^{4}-x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486407 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "881251ae-3ce1-502c-8f83-ef36164a7a11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486407 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+1}{3{x}^{4}-x+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{7x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{3-0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486408 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486408 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486408 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486408 #edb/ref 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+9}{8{x}^{5}+x}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486408 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8bd57c14-ae43-573a-96f9-4a756bc9a869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486408 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+9}{8{x}^{5}+x} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{8{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{5}})}{(8+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{8+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486415 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486415 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{3}-x}{{x}^{2}+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9426b3d5-4ce9-5f76-8b8a-628c207151a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486415 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{3}-x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6x-\\frac{1}{x})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6x-0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & =6{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
